    "case_id": "27",
    "case_summary": "A 45-year-old Nepalese woman reported experiencing acute pain and reduced vision in both eyes for the past two months. She had been treated with oral acetazolamide and 4% pilocarpine drops, and had undergone laser iridotomy in the left eye. There was no prior history of similar episodes. Upon examination, both eyes had light perception vision, with intraocular pressure (IOP) measured at 50 mmHg in the right eye and 54 mmHg in the left eye. The left eye exhibited a mid-peripheral patent laser iridotomy. The anterior chambers were nearly flat, with diffuse pigmentation observed on the posterior corneal and anterior lens surfaces. Both eyes displayed signs of acute angle closure, including iris atrophy, glaucomflecken, prominent iris vessels, and closed angles on indentation gonioscopy. Retinoscopy was attempted but was unsuccessful due to poor fundal glow caused by diffuse pigmentation on the posterior corneal surface. The axial length was measured at 21.63 mm in the right eye and 22.52 mm in the left eye.\n\nUltrasound Biomicroscopy (UBM) showed an anteriorly displaced crystalline lens with extensive irido-lenticular contact and peripheral anterior synechiae (PAS) closing the angles completely in both eyes. B-Scan ultrasonography showed normal posterior segments in both eyes.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
